<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">An Account of Corsica</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1768</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An Account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> is a book by the Scottish writer James Boswell, published in 1768, describing the island and his visit in 1765 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasquale-paoli">Pasquale Paoli</a>, leader of the Corsican Republic. Its portrait of Paoli as a heroic patriot made him famous across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and won public sympathy for Corsican independence. Widely read and translated, it established Boswell's literary reputation long before his Life of Johnson.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/an-account-of-corsica.jpg" alt="An Account of Corsica" width="320" height="566" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Boswell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_Account_of_Corsica,_title_page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
